WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
KASLIN FIELDS:
Today, we're going

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.570
to explore what it's like
to create your first Google

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.310
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.170
In our GKE Essentials
Cluster Modes video,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.770
we went over the differences
between GKE standard mode

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.290
and GKE Autopilot mode clusters.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.300
Now we'll put that
knowledge into action

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.770
by creating one of each.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.660
I'm Kaslin Fields,
and I'm a developer

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.580
advocate here at
Google Cloud, where

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.800
I focus on Google
Kubernetes Engine, as well

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.110
as the open-source
Kubernetes project.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.645
So today, we're going to
explore creating your first GKE

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:29.145
cluster.

00:00:29.145 --> 00:00:32.418
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.350
We're going to
deploy our clusters

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.720
today using the
Google Cloud Console.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.870
There are many ways to deploy
and manage your clusters,

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.330
but the Console is a great
place to start if you

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.490
want to learn what's possible.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.640
From here, we're going
to go to the navigation

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.090
menu on the upper left
and find Kubernetes

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.600
Engine in the Compute section.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.850
You could also
use the search bar

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.209
at the top and search for
Kubernetes or Google Kubernetes

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.200
Engine.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.320
Now we're in the GKE
section of the Console.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.240
Since Autopilot mode is
still relatively new,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.820
you can see some
information here

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.670
about what Autopilot mode
is, along with an option

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.730
to try a demo, where you
will create an Autopilot mode

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.700
cluster and deploy
a workload into it.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.150
Below that, you'll see
any existing GKE clusters,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.870
along with information
about them,

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:32.290
such as their Name, Location,
Mode, and Resources.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.130
Let's try to create a cluster
by clicking on the Create button

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.550
at the top of this page.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.920
The Console prompts us
by asking whether we

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.760
would like to create a Standard
mode cluster or an Autopilot

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.160
mode cluster, as
well as providing

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.500
a link to additional information
about the differences.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.970
So let's start by creating
an Autopilot mode cluster.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.210
On this page, there's
some information

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.700
about what creating an Autopilot
mode cluster will give you,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.790
and there are some
input fields where

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.850
we will need to specify
information about our cluster.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.370
First, we can specify a Name.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.580
Then, what Region we would
like to run this cluster in.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:20.650
Remember, all Autopilot mode GKE
clusters are regional clusters,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.140
meaning they're deployed
across the zones that

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.610
make up a region, so there's
no need to specify a zone here.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.640
You could simply hit Create
at the bottom of the page

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.860
to create the cluster
at this point,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.170
but let's take a look at
the additional options

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.080
that you may wish to configure.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.410
If we scroll down, we can
see some expandable sections

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.800
for Networking and
Advanced Options.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.160
In Networking, if you choose
to create a public cluster,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.870
then your nodes
will be provisioned

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.550
with externally
accessible IP addresses

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.760
so that you could reach
the nodes from anywhere

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.230
over the internet.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:01.450
You can also enable control
plane authorized networks

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.190
in order to block
untrusted, non-GCP source

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.290
IPs from accessing
the Kubernetes

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.350
master through HTTPS.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.920
A private cluster will be
one where the nodes are only

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.150
accessible via private IPs
on Google Cloud's network.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.190
The nodes of a
private cluster only

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.240
communicate via the private
Google Cloud network.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.110
You can configure Cloud Network
Address Translation, or Cloud

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.990
NAT, for your nodes
in order for them

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.740
to send outbound
requests to the internet.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:40.150
In Advanced options,
if we scroll down here,

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.970
you can see
configuration options

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:46.480
for the Release channel
for your GKE cluster.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.360
When you enroll a new
cluster in a release channel,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.050
Google automatically manages
the version and upgrade cadence

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.690
for that cluster
and its node pools.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.010
Your choice of release channel
between Rapid, and Regular,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:03.850
and Stable will
affect how quickly

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.920
new versions of
Kubernetes become

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.380
available for your
cluster to upgrade

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.540
and how often your
cluster must upgrade

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.540
to stay in compliance with the
currently supported versions

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.770
of Kubernetes.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.300
So for this cluster,
I'm just going

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.720
to go with the default,
which is the Regular channel.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.480
If you'll need to specify
acceptable maintenance windows

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.780
for your GKE cluster, you
can select Enable maintenance

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.860
window here.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.580
You must allow at least 48 hours
of maintenance availability

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.180
in a 32-day rolling window,
and a maintenance window

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.660
must allot at least four hours
in order to be considered.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.570
These options help you
provide optimal availability

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.670
of your cluster,
while allowing Google

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.310
to ensure that your clusters
are kept up-to-date with

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:48.580
supported Kubernetes
patches and upgrades.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.190
You can also specify
Metadata and Labels

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:55.120
in order to keep your
cluster resources organized.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.890
The Description
field here can be

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.620
used to provide a description
for this cluster's purpose

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.770
or any other information you
wish to use to describe it.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.950
For example, I
could put in GKEE--

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:07.795
GKE Essentials-- cluster.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.790
Labels can be used to
help organize resources

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.300
within your project.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.030
For example, you
could use labels,

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.090
such as prod, dev,
or test, to indicate

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.830
the use of different clusters.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.380
Or you could specify a
team name to indicate

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.960
that this cluster is intended
for use by that team.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.610
There are many
ways that you could

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.930
use labels and metadata to keep
your Cloud resources organized.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:35.240
Now at the bottom
of this page, we

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.390
can use the Create button to
create the cluster that we've

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.840
configured, or Cancel to exit
the page without creating

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:42.960
the cluster.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.330
You can also use the REST
or Command Line options here

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.020
to get the REST API
call or command for use

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.590
with the gcloud CLI in order
to create this cluster using

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.970
the CLI or other automation.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:02.040
So let's create this cluster now
using the Create button here.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.010
So you'll see that our
cluster is now creating.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.940
Its status is this
currently being

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.340
created rolling circle here.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.900
You'll also notice here
at the upper right,

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.170
you have a notification
icon, where

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:17.570
the circle is going, as well.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.690
When our Autopilot mode GKE
cluster has finished creating,

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:24.260
we'll receive a notification
on this notification icon,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.480
indicating that the
cluster is ready.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.300
Now let's go back
to Create, where

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:34.370
we will explore configuring
a standard mode GKE cluster.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.280
You can see that the format
of the page for creating

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.900
a standard mode cluster is
already a little bit different.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.790
The sections you'll
need to go through

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.310
to configure your GKE
standard mode cluster

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.860
are displayed on the
left-hand side here.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.200
The center contains
the input fields,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.050
where you'll specify your
configuration information.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.390
And the right-hand
side gives a summary

00:06:56.390 --> 00:07:00.200
of the cluster you'll
create, including any Cluster

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.380
set-up guides used, Immutable
properties that you won't

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:07.130
be able to change once the
cluster is created, billing

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.710
information about the
cluster that you're

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.580
going to be creating and its
resources, any Beta properties

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.790
that you'll be using,
the Limitations

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:20.120
on the size of the cluster,
as well as information

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.390
about the Node pools and
the actual instances that

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.860
will make up your cluster.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.730
So our first tasks,
again, are to select

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.910
a Name for the cluster
and a Zone or a Region.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:35.090
While Autopilot mode clusters
can only be regional,

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.310
standard mode
clusters may also be

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.790
configured to run in only a
single zone within a region.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.090
So you can learn more
about the differences

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:47.690
between zonal and regional
clusters in our documentation.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.540
For this cluster, I'll just
make it a Zonal cluster

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.970
with the default here.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:58.670
Again, you'll need to choose
a Release channel to determine

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:01.100
the frequency of Kubernetes
patches and upgrades.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.010
However, in standard
mode, you may also

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.890
choose to create a GKE
control plane using

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.590
a Static version of Kubernetes.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:12.800
Using a static version will
require taking responsibility

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.780
for managing your
cluster's version

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.810
and ensuring the cluster is
using a supported version

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.580
of Kubernetes.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.520
Now we could stop here
and create our cluster,

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.890
but let's quickly review
the additional configuration

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.400
options for a standard
mode GKE cluster.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.770
In the Node Pools
section over here,

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.860
you can configure the
number of worker nodes

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.679
for your GKE cluster.

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.710
You can enable
autoscaling, as well as

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:43.669
node auto-upgrade
and node auto-repair.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:48.620
You can also select the Number
of nodes for your default node

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.110
pool.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.960
In the Nodes section within
the default node pool,

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:56.540
we can see more information
about the nodes themselves,

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:00.950
such as choosing the machine
type, as well as the image--

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:05.450
the operating system that will
be configured on those nodes.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.780
In Security, you can
configure Service accounts,

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:13.430
Cloud API Access scopes,
and Shielded node options.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.160
In the Metadata
section here, you

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:19.730
can add labels specifically
for the nodes of your cluster,

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.680
rather than labels
that will be applied

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.570
to the cluster as a whole.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:26.300
You can also specify
Kubernetes Node taints

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:28.880
at this stage to help you
manage what workloads will be

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.240
able to run on this node pool.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.570
Within the Cluster
section, you can

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:39.200
configure cluster-level
settings pertaining

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.560
to Automation,
Networking, Security,

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.750
Metadata, and additional
cluster Features.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.200
So in the Automation
section is where

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.630
you can configure
maintenance windows, as we

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.420
did in Autopilot mode, as well.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:56.120
In the Cluster
Networking section,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:59.420
you can configure public
or private clusters,

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.130
as we saw in Autopilot mode.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.410
However, standard
mode also includes

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.630
some additional advanced
networking options,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.580
so you might want to
take a look at those.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:14.490
And in security, you can enable
shielded or Confidential nodes,

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.810
secret encryption,
Workload identity,

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:21.900
Google Groups for RBAC, and some
Legacy security options here.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.890
In Metadata, you can configure
cluster-level Description data

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.430
and Labels, as we saw
with Autopilot mode.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.770
And in Features,
you can configure

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.810
additional Google
Cloud features,

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:37.740
such as Cloud Run for
Anthos, Enabling Cloud

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.830
Logging and Monitoring, and
additional capabilities,

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.510
such as Cloud TPUs,
Istio, and more.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.010
Again, at the
bottom of this page,

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.800
once you've configured
all of your options,

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.490
you can Create the cluster
that you've configured,

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.810
you can Cancel, or you can get
the REST or gcloud CLI command

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.370
to create this cluster.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:06.660
Now I'll choose to Create and
begin creation of this cluster.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.330
The cluster will take
several minutes to create,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:14.130
as Google Cloud is spinning
up the compute resources that

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.460
configure the cluster--

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.240
and configures the
cluster according

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.490
to your specifications.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:24.630
You can see that the Autopilot
mode cluster is still

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.360
working on completing here.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.360
Once it's done, it will be
indicated by a checkmark,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.180
as you see here on
the existing clusters.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.860
And you may also notice that
in an Autopilot mode cluster,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.803
there are no number
of nodes indicated.

00:11:41.803 --> 00:11:43.470
And for the one that's
creating, there's

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.020
no VCPU or memory
indicated because there

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.775
are no workloads running on it.

00:11:47.775 --> 00:11:49.650
So when you do have a
workload running on it,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.618
it will indicate how many VCPU
and memory are being used.

00:11:52.618 --> 00:11:54.660
But you won't be able to
see the number of nodes,

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.968
because in Autopilot mode,
the nodes of your Kubernetes

00:11:57.968 --> 00:11:59.760
cluster are not accessible
and you will not

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.620
be able to see them
in the console.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.160
And as you can see from
the existing Standard Mode

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.560
cluster in this project,
when the Standard Mode

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:12.150
cluster finishes its creation,
you'll see the Number of nodes,

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:15.240
as well as the Total
amount of VCPU and memory

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.870
that are available to workloads
running on that cluster.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.460
There's also a
notification here that

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.910
indicates that I'm not
running enough workloads to be

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:27.250
using this cluster efficiently.

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:30.720
So Autopilot Mode provides
a streamlined GKE cluster

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.270
creation process
with smart defaults,

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.070
applying Google's
recommended best

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.950
practices for high
availability and securing

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.050
hardening for your cluster.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.800
The pay-per-workload model
of GKE Autopilot clusters

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.400
may also help increase
resource usage efficiency,

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.000
and thus optimize costs as
you pay only for the resources

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.410
you use.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.260
The higher degree of management
provided by Autopilot mode

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.860
results in a cluster creation
process with fewer overall

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:00.430
configuration options--

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.250
the goal being to
get you to the stage

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:06.690
where you can run your
workloads on Kubernetes faster.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:10.980
Standard mode clusters offer
more flexibility and control,

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.470
but also require you
to think more carefully

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.630
about the many
considerations that

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:20.040
can go into your GKE Standard
Mode cluster's configuration.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.860
Whether Autopilot or
Standard Mode, GKE

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.600
aims to provide
you with the tools

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.660
you need to be successful
running your applications

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.150
on Kubernetes and Google Cloud.

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.298
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:13:32.298 --> 00:13:33.840
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:37.170
